  \toprule\noalign{}
  X \textbackslash Y & 1 & 2 \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  1 & 0.19 & 0.01 \\
  2 & 0.13 & 0.01 \\
  4 & 0.05 & 0.61 \\
  \end{longtable}

  Qual a covariância entre as variáveis \(X\) e \(Y\)?

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumii{(\alph{enumii})}
  \item
    \(-4.144\)
  \item
    \(0.496\)
  \item
    \(5.590\)
  \item
    \(0.504\)
  \item
    \(0.302\)
  \end{enumerate}

  \textbf{Solution}\\
  Primeiramente devemos completar a tabela com as probabilidades
  marginais de \(X\) e \(Y\):

  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}cccc@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  X Y & 1 & 2 & P(X=x) \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  1 & 0.19 & 0.01 & 0.2 \\
  2 & 0.13 & 0.01 & 0.14 \\
  4 & 0.05 & 0.61 & 0.66 \\
  P(Y=y) & 0.37 & 0.63 & 1 \\
  \end{longtable}

  Em seguida, calculamos

  \[\begin{aligned}
  E(X) &=& 1\times0.2+2\times0.14+4\times0.66 = 3.12\\
  E(Y) &=& 1\times0.37+2\times0.63 = 1.63\\
  \end{aligned}\]

  A distribuição do produto é dada por

  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}ccccc@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  k & 1 & 2 & 4 & 8 \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  P(XY = k) & 0.19 & 0.14 & 0.06 & 0.61 \\
  \end{longtable}

  De modo que
  \[E(XY) = 1\times0.19+2\times0.14+4\times0.06+8\times0.61 = 5.59\] e,
  portanto, \[\text{Cov}(X,Y) = E(XY)-E(X)E(Y) = 0.504.\]

